export const meta = {
  name: 'understand-brain-and-benchmarks',
  description: 'Comprehend entire-brain, entire graph (sem) internals, both benchmarks; investigate sonnet +graph accuracy drop; plan a safe audited sonnet 30/300 re-run',
  phases: [
    { title: 'Understand', detail: '4 parallel readers: brain system, entire-sem graph internals, brain-bench, injection+accuracy+collision' },
    { title: 'Synthesize', detail: 'merge briefs + known harness facts into one packet + safe run plan' },
  ],
}

const RO = 'STRICT READ-ONLY. Do NOT modify, create, delete, or run any state-writing command (no eval runs, no builds that persist). Read/search/--help only. Final text is raw structured data for synthesis — terse markdown, file:line pointers, no fluff.'

const readers = [
  {
    label: 'brain-system',
    prompt: `${RO}

TASK: Locate + explain the "entire-brain" knowledge system behind the MCP tools brain_search, brain_code, brain_impact, brain_context, brain_query, brain_vsearch, brain_status.
Search roots: /Users/suhaan/Documents/Coding/ (dirs: brain-atlas, _entire-brain-handoff-archive, any brain*), /Users/suhaan/.local/share/entire/ (plugins/bin holds entire-sem — is there an entire-brain binary too?), /Users/suhaan/devenv/ + its worktrees, and ~/.claude.json (find the entire-brain MCP server registration: what binary/command/path launches it, what args/env).
Explain: (a) what the brain IS and does; (b) its layers — durable "facts" vs "semantic"/code-graph (per brain_status which reports which sources are live); (c) storage: vector store? sqlite? ndjson on disk? where; (d) how it ingests/builds a repo; (e) relationship to the \`entire sem\` code graph — does brain wrap sem, or are they separate. Give binary paths + file:line for core pieces.`,
  },
  {
    label: 'entire-sem-graph',
    prompt: `${RO}

TASK: Explain HOW the \`entire sem\` code-graph is generated internally + its per-language coverage.
Binary: /Users/suhaan/.local/share/entire/plugins/bin/entire-sem — run READ-ONLY: \`entire-sem --help\`, \`entire-sem symbols --help\`, \`entire-sem edges --help\`, \`entire-sem version\`. Find its source if present (search /Users/suhaan/devenv/cli and /Users/suhaan/Documents/Coding for the sem/graph/semantic builder).
Determine: (a) parser technology — tree-sitter? LSP? ast-grep? which per language; (b) which of the 9 SWE-bench-Multilingual languages (C, C++, Go, Java, JavaScript, PHP, Ruby, Rust, TypeScript) are well-supported vs weak/unsupported — this explains why some prebuilt graphs are "degraded/unsafe"; (c) node/symbol types + edge types (DEFINES/CONTAINS/IMPORTS/EXTENDS/CALLS…) + the ndjson record schema; (d) the CALLS confidence heuristic (~0.62 fixed conf mentioned). Also \`head\` /Users/suhaan/Documents/Coding/brain-swebench/graphs/COMPLETENESS.tsv to report which repos/langs came out degraded vs complete.`,
  },
  {
    label: 'brain-bench',
    prompt: `${RO}

TASK: Explain /Users/suhaan/Documents/Coding/brain-bench (the "competitive-eval" benchmark — DISTINCT from brain-swebench).
Read: README.md, bench.py, run.py, tasks.py, bench_tasks.py, the \`oracle\` binary's purpose, oracle-py / oracle-ts / oracle-rust-rustc dirs, repos*.json, results*/ layout, docs/, competitive-eval-manifest.json (structure/size only — do NOT dump it).
Explain: what it measures (code-graph retrieval accuracy vs competitor tools? symbol-resolution correctness? go-to-def recall?); what "oracle" provides (ground truth?); the scoring method; languages covered; and how its PURPOSE differs from brain-swebench (which measures downstream Claude issue-fix rate). One-paragraph bottom line + key file:line.`,
  },
  {
    label: 'injection-accuracy-collision',
    prompt: `${RO}

Deep-dive + audit on /Users/suhaan/Documents/Coding/brain-swebench.
KNOWN (do NOT re-derive): treated arm appends a GQ_NOTE prompt describing a graph-query/gq tool (def/show/callers/callees/symbols/file/stats) backed by prebuilt graph.symbols.ndjson + graph.edges.ndjson; baseline gets no tool; eval is official SWE-bench Docker harness (v2 suffix = git-apply --check validated, strictest); ~22/30 shipped graphs were degraded/unsafe.

JOB 1 — accuracy investigation: WHY might sonnet "+graph" resolve FEWER than baseline (observed 27/30=90% baseline vs 23/30=77% +graph) yet cost ~11-15% less? Read run_cc_one.sh (full GQ_NOTE text), the gq wrapper script, swebench_treated.yaml, measure_sweep.py; cross-reference results/sonnet/baseline vs results/sonnet/treated (which specific instance_ids flipped from resolved→unresolved), the eval logs, and graphs/COMPLETENESS.tsv (were the flipped tasks on degraded graphs?). Produce concrete, evidence-backed hypotheses for the 4-task drop: (a) gq tool-calls consume turns/tokens leaving fewer of the max-50 turns for the actual fix; (b) degraded graphs return wrong/incomplete answers → worse patches; (c) "prefer graph INSTEAD OF grep/cat" makes it skip context grep would've found; (d) n=30 noise (a 4-task swing); (e) arm-asymmetric patch pollution surviving clean/v2. For each: state evidence found + the exact check to confirm.

JOB 2 — collision check for a NEW run: the full matrix (models=[haiku sonnet opus] arms=[baseline treated] conc=2) is RUNNING NOW (haiku baseline live; it WILL reach the sonnet cell later). If we launch a SEPARATE audited sonnet 30-task run now, does it collide? Inspect .locks/ (contents + how locks are scoped/named), how run_cc_one.sh / run_matrix.sh choose checkouts/<model>/... worktree dirs and results/<model>/<arm>/ output paths, and whether a manual sonnet-30 invocation would clobber the matrix's own sonnet cell or share worktrees/locks/preds. Verdict: safe-parallel / must-isolate-dir / must-wait — with the exact mechanism, and the safest concrete command(s) to run 30 sonnet tasks (both arms) through the OFFICIAL Docker eval track without disturbing the live matrix. Quote decisive lines.`,
  },
]

phase('Understand')
const raw = await parallel(readers.map((r) => () => agent(r.prompt, { label: r.label, phase: 'Understand', model: 'sonnet', effort: 'high' })))
const briefs = raw.map((t, i) => ({ label: readers[i].label, text: t })).filter((x) => x.text)

phase('Synthesize')
const KNOWN = [
  'PRIOR HARNESS ANALYSIS (treat as established, integrate — do not contradict without evidence):',
  '- brain-swebench has TWO solver tracks sharing one eval: Track A = local Claude Code CLI (claude -p, host git worktrees) driven by run_cc_one.sh / run_matrix.sh over {haiku,sonnet,opus}x{baseline,treated} — this is the LIVE running matrix. Track B = official mini-swe-agent swebench harness in Docker, sonnet only, run_pilot.sh + swebench_treated.yaml, outputs results/official_sonnet/.',
  '- baseline vs treated differ ONLY by an appended GQ_NOTE describing a graph-query tool + access to prebuilt entire-sem graph (symbols+edges ndjson) for the repo@base_commit; retrieval interface, not a context dump. Track A skips treated entirely if the graph is missing (never silently degrades to baseline).',
  '- Graph build: entire sem symbols; entire sem edges | filter_edges.py (CALLS heuristic conf 0.62, quadratic; filtered CAP_OUT=6/CAP_IN=6 for OOM-safety). graph_stats.py validate flags truncated (fail) vs degraded/unsafe (disclosed). COMPLETENESS.tsv discloses per-instance; ~22/30 were degraded/unsafe.',
  '- Eval = official python -m swebench.harness.run_evaluation --dataset swe-bench/SWE-bench_Multilingual, Docker, real FAIL_TO_PASS/PASS_TO_PASS. goldcheck (jqlang/jq-2235 gold patch -> resolved=1) confirms the eval mechanism itself is sound.',
  '- Methodology suffixes: raw -> _clean (clean_patches.py strips new-files/lockfiles/binary/vendor) -> _v2 (validate_assemble.py + git apply --check). Resolved DROPS raw17->clean12->v2:10 for haiku/baseline as pollution removed; v2 is the trustworthy number. score_matrix.py refuses to compare mismatched suffixes across arms.',
  '- fair_tokens.py: reporting-only. real=cache_creation+input+output (+ $ cost = ground truth) is the unbiased headline over the common run set; matched-both-patched set is labeled collider-bias diagnostic only.',
].join('\n')

const packet = briefs.map((x) => `### ${x.label}\n${x.text}`).join('\n\n---\n\n')

const synth = await agent(
  `You are the synthesis stage. Merge the reader briefs below WITH the prior harness analysis into ONE tight technical understanding packet for an orchestrator who will then run an audited sonnet re-run. Integrate; resolve overlaps; flag any contradiction between a brief and the prior analysis.

Output these sections (markdown, dense, specific with paths/script names — no fluff):
1. **entire-brain** — what it is; facts vs semantic layers; storage; how it ingests a repo; its relation to entire sem.
2. **entire graph (\`entire sem\`)** — parser tech, per-language strength/weakness across the 9 swebench langs, node/edge schema, the CALLS heuristic; and concretely WHICH langs/repos produced degraded graphs (from COMPLETENESS.tsv).
3. **The benchmarks** — brain-swebench (recap: 2 tracks, pipeline, official eval, v2 methodology) + brain-bench (competitive-eval: what/how, one paragraph).
4. **Treated injection mechanism** — exactly what +graph feeds the solver and how it's queried.
5. **Accuracy verdict — sonnet 90%→77%** — is the +graph resolve drop most likely REAL or ARTIFACT? Rank the hypotheses by evidence; name the specific flipped instance_ids and whether they sat on degraded graphs; give the single most decisive confirming check.
6. **SAFE-RUN PLAN** — how to run a fully-audited OFFICIAL 30/300 sonnet short run (both arms) WITHOUT disturbing the live haiku/sonnet/opus matrix: exact script/commands, isolated dirs if required, the collision verdict + mechanism, and rough $ cost + wall-clock. Prefer the official Docker Track B for "fully audited."

${KNOWN}

READER BRIEFS:

${packet}`,
  { label: 'synthesis', phase: 'Synthesize', effort: 'high' },
)

return synth

